ity of Manchester [Input: Search...]\nL5: \nL6: # Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture\nL7: \nL8:   * Glyn Hobbs\nL9:   * , Catherine M. Frazer\nL10:   * , David C J Gardner\nL11:   * , John A. Cullum\nL12:   * , Stephen G. Oliver\nL13: \nL14: Research output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\nL15: ## Abstract\nL16: The study of the physiology of the filamentous bacterium Streptomyces is inhibited by its formation of mycelial pellets in liquid cultures. It is demonstrated that dispersed growth may be achieved by the addition of polymers to the culture medium. Uncharged polymers, such as polyethylene glycol, are relatively ineffective but polyanions such as agar, Carbopol and Junlon produce dispersed cultures when included in a defined growth medium at low concentrations.\nL17: Junlon-containing media enable optical density measurements to be used to follow batch growth of Streptomyces. Improvements in both biomass yield and product yield of the pigmented antibiotic actinorhodin were found to result from the incorporation of Junlon into minimal medium. © 1989 Springer-Verlag.\nL18: Original language  | English\nL19: Pages (from-to)  | 272-277\nL20: Number of pages  | 5\nL21: Journal  | 【4†Applied microbiology and biotechnology】\nL22: Volume  | 31\nL23: Issue number  | 3\nL24: DOIs  |\nL25: \nL26:   * 【5†https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00258408†doi.org】\nL27: Publication status  | Published - Sept 1989\nL28: ## Research Highlights, Institutes and Platforms\nL29: \nL30:   * Manchester Institute of Biotechnology\nL31: \nL32: ## Access to Document\nL33: \nL34:   * 【5†10.1007/BF00258408†doi.org】\nL35: \nL36: ## Fingerprint\nL37: \nL38: Dive into the research topics of 'Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\nL39: \nL40: 【6†View full fingerprint】\nL41: ## Cite this\nL42: \nL43:   * APA\nL44:   * Author\nL45:   * BIBTEX\nL46:   * Harvard\nL47:   * Standard\nL48:   * RIS\nL49:   * Vancouver\nL50: \