liver, SG 1989, '【7†Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture】', Applied microbiology and biotechnology, vol. 31, no. 3, pp. 272-277. 【5†https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00258408†doi.org】\nL83: \nL84: 【7†Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture.】 / Hobbs, Glyn; Frazer, Catherine M.; Gardner, David C J et al.\nL85: In: Applied microbiology and biotechnology, Vol. 31, No. 3, 09.1989, p. 272-277.\nL86: \nL87: Research output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\nL88: TY - JOUR\nL89: \nL90: T1 - Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture\nL91: \nL92: AU - Hobbs, Glyn\nL93: \nL94: AU - Frazer, Catherine M.\nL95: \nL96: AU - Gardner, David C J\nL97: \nL98: AU - Cullum, John A.\nL99: \nL100: AU - Oliver, Stephen G.\nL101: \nL102: PY - 1989/9\nL103: \nL104: Y1 - 1989/9\nL105: N2 - The study of the physiology of the filamentous bacterium Streptomyces is inhibited by its formation of mycelial pellets in liquid cultures. It is demonstrated that dispersed growth may be achieved by the addition of polymers to the culture medium. Uncharged polymers, such as polyethylene glycol, are relatively ineffective but polyanions such as agar, Carbopol and Junlon produce dispersed cultures when included in a defined growth medium at low concentrations.\nL106: Junlon-containing media enable optical density measurements to be used to follow batch growth of Streptomyces. Improvements in both biomass yield and product yield of the pigmented antibiotic actinorhodin were found to result from the incorporation of Junlon into minimal medium. © 1989 Springer-Verlag.\nL107: AB - The study of the physiology of the filamentous bacterium Streptomyces is inhibited by its formation of mycelial pellets in liquid cultures. It is demonstrated that dispersed growth may be achieved by the addition of polymers to the culture medium. Uncharged polymers, such as polyethylene glycol, are relatively ineffective but polyanions such as agar, Carbopol and Junlon produce dispersed cultures when included in a defined growth medium at low concentrations.\nL108: Junlon-containing media enable optical density measurements to be used to follow batch growth of Streptomyces. Improvements in both biomass yield and product yield of the pigmented antibiotic actinorhodin were found to result from the incorporation of Junlon into minimal medium. © 1989 Springer-Verlag.\nL109: U2 - 10.1007/BF00258408\nL110: \nL111: DO - 10.1007/BF00258408\nL112: \nL113: M3 - Article\nL114: \nL115: SN - 0175-7598\nL116: \nL117: VL - 31\nL118: \nL119: SP - 272\nL120: \nL121: EP - 277\nL122: \nL123: JO - Applied microbiology and biotechnology\nL124: \nL125: JF - Applied microbiology and biotechnology\nL126: \nL127: IS - 3\nL128: \nL129: ER -\nL130: \nL131: Hobbs G, Frazer CM, Gardner DCJ, Cullum JA, Oliver SG. 【7†Dispersed growth of Streptomyces in liquid culture】. Applied microbiology and biotechnology. 1989 Sept;31(3):272-277. doi: 10.1007/BF00258408\nL132: Powered by 【8†Pure Link opens in a new tab†www.elsevier.com】, 【9†Scopus Link opens in a new tab†www.scopus.com】 & 【10†Elsevier Fingerprint Engine™ Link opens in a new tab†www.elsevier.com】\nL133: \nL134: All content on this site: Copyright © 2026 Research Explorer The University of Manchester, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\nL135: We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the 【11†use of cookies】\nL136: \nL137: 【12†Research Explorer The University of Manchester data protection policy   Link opens in a new tab†www.manchester.ac.uk】\nL138: \nL139: 【13†About web accessibility   Link opens in a new tab】\nL140: \nL141: 【14†Report vulnerability   Link opens in a new tab†elsevier.responsibledisclosure.com】\n",
